### The Five Principles

| Letter | Principle | Core Idea |
|--------|-----------|-----------|
| **S** | **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)** | A class, module, or function should have only one reason to change, meaning it should do one thing. |
| **O** | **Open-Closed Principle (OCP)** | Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification. |
| **L** | **Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)** | Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting correctness. |
| **I** | **Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)** | Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use; keep interfaces small and focused. |
| **D** | **Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)** | High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. |

These principles are widely adopted as best practices for writing clean, maintainable object-oriented code. Following them can result in a larger initial codebase, but significantly reduces the risk of bugs and makes future changes easier to implement. [1](#14ffcdd0-5849-44d1-9af3-8226af5f1cb7)

